-- The target language of the extractor: the small Java fragment `ToJava.ard`
-- generates, as an AST rather than source text. It defines the subset we commit
-- to, it is what a future semantics and correctness statement are given for, and
-- it keeps spelling in one trusted place (`JavaPrint.ard`).
--
-- SCOPE. Exactly what the generator emits: no operators, loops, conditionals,
-- generics, interfaces, `void`, array types or entry point, and no modifier but
-- `public static`. Every value has static type `Object` and is cast where needed,
-- so this is not Featherweight Java. Classes come in three shapes: the program
-- class, the anonymous one-method closures (`jClosure`), and the local classes
-- that tie a `fix` knot (`jLocalClass`).
--
-- The runtime (`runtime/Rt.java`) is hand-written. Its CORE has dedicated nodes
-- (RUNTIME ABI below), being what a correctness statement quantifies over; its
-- periphery is referenced by name from `JavaAxioms.ard`, being axioms a proof
-- assumes rather than interprets.
--
-- `JExpr` and `JStmt` are mutually recursive sorts, mirroring the generator's
-- `\Sigma (Array JStmt) JExpr` -- a statement prelude plus a value. Binders are
-- NAMES: the `path`-derived names are what lets the generator's recursion pass
-- Arend's termination checker without threading state.
--
-- ARRAY-OF-\Sigma, NOT ARRAY-OF-RECORD in `jClosure`, `jSwitch` and
-- `jLocalClass`: a record with a `JStmt` field would join the JExpr/JStmt `\data`
-- cycle, which Arend rejects. (`LambdaBox.ard`'s `case` branches likewise.)
--
-- Compare: Malfunction.v, the verified λ□ backend (its `Ident.t`/`Longident.t`
-- split is `JIdent`/`JQIdent`, its `Mvar`/`Mglobal` split is `jVar`/`jStatic`);
-- CompCert `Clight.v` (same two-sort shape, and the same printer-outside-the-
-- proof division CertiCoq's C backend uses); Featherweight Java (`x | e.f |
-- e.m(e*) | new C(e*) | (C)e` is the expression core); Jinja/µJava (folds
-- statements into expressions, we do not).

\import Data.Bool
\import Data.Maybe
\import Data.String

-- Both `String`: a simple Java identifier, and a possibly dotted one (`Rt.Fn`,
-- `Long.valueOf`). Every such position holds an already-valid name -- mangling
-- happens once, at `mangleKername`. Plain strings are what source-level backends
-- use (MetaRocq, lean-to-lambdabox); interned integers (CompCert, CertiCoq λANF)
-- buy cheap maps and freshness proofs this generator does not need.
\func JIdent => String

\func JQIdent => String

-- The runtime's class name lives here because the ABI nodes below carry no name
-- of their own, and this decides how they print.
\func runtimeClassName : JIdent => "Rt"

\func runtimeQual : String => runtimeClassName ++ "."

-- `tFn`/`tData` are separate from `tClass` although they print as `Rt.Fn` and
-- `Rt.Data`: a semantics has to give them meaning (a function space, a tagged
-- product), not treat them as opaque named classes.
\data JType
  | tObject
  | tFn    -- the runtime's one-method function interface: λ□'s function space
  | tData  -- the runtime's constructor representation: a tag plus fields
  | tClass JQIdent

\data JExpr
  | jVar JIdent  -- a local or parameter as a value: `p3`, `l7`
  -- A qualified name, as a value or a static receiver. Apart from `jVar` because
  -- it is resolved globally, not in the environment.
  | jStatic JQIdent
  | jField JExpr JIdent  -- `e.f`: `Rt.BOX`, `Rt.PRIM_ADD_LONG`
  -- Printed fully parenthesized (see JavaPrint.ard); unavoidable, since every
  -- value is typed `Object`.
  | jCast JType JExpr
  | jInvoke JExpr JIdent (Array JExpr)  -- `e.m(args)`
  | jCallStatic JQIdent (Array JExpr)  -- `c_foo()`, `Long.valueOf(5L)`
  | jNew JType (Array JExpr)  -- an exception, or a `fix` local class

  -- --- RUNTIME ABI ---------------------------------------------------------
  -- λ□'s `box`, `app`, `lambda`, `construct` and the tag/field reads of `case`
  -- and `proj`: the nodes a correctness statement is about, hence nodes and not
  -- combinations of the general ones above over hard-coded member names. As
  -- strings, the AST could not tell the runtime's application from any call of a
  -- method named `apply`. What `apply` DOES is still fixed by `Rt.java` and
  -- assumed; the point is having a node to assume it of.
  | jBox  -- `Rt.BOX`: an erased proof or type
  | jApply JExpr JExpr  -- `((Rt.Fn)(f)).apply(x)`; the cast is how application is spelled
  -- `new Rt.Fn(){ public Object apply(Object <p>){ <body> } }` -- an anonymous
  -- class, no `invokedynamic`. Always `public Object apply(Object)`, so the
  -- method is (parameter, body); see ARRAY-OF-\Sigma above.
  | jClosure JIdent (Array JStmt)
  | jDataNew Nat (Array JExpr)  -- `new Rt.Data(tag, new Object[]{ .. })`
  | jDataTag JExpr  -- `d.tag`: the `switch` scrutinee a λ□ `case` becomes
  -- `d.fields[i]`. `Nat`, not `JExpr`: λ□ has no computed field access, and
  -- folding `proj`'s `npars + idx` here is why the fragment needs no arithmetic.
  | jDataField JExpr Nat
  -- --- end RUNTIME ABI -----------------------------------------------------

  -- `C.this` -- the enclosing instance of local class `C`, from inside a nested
  -- anonymous class: a `fix` sibling is referenced within a `jClosure` whose own
  -- `this` is the closure. NOT `jStatic "C.this"`, which prints identically:
  -- `jStatic` is resolved globally, this one in the enclosing class's scope.
  | jQualThis JQIdent
  | jStringLit String  -- the printer quotes but does not escape
  | jIntLit Nat  -- a constructor tag or a field index
  | jLongLit Int  -- a `long` (`5L`) for λ□ `prim` ints; signed, so `Int`

\data JStmt
  -- `final Object l3 = e;` / `Object r3;`. Uninitialized is exactly the `case`
  -- result variable, assigned in every branch.
  | jLocal (isFinal : Bool) JType JIdent (Maybe JExpr)
  | jAssign JExpr JExpr  -- only ever that `case` result variable
  -- The printer emits each case's block and `break`, so there is no fallthrough.
  -- Cases are \Sigma-shaped (see above), as in Malfunction's `Mswitch`.
  | jSwitch JExpr (Array (\Sigma Nat (Array JStmt))) (Array JStmt)
  -- A local class -- declared in a block, capturing effectively-final locals --
  -- whose k methods may call each other. This is how λ□ `fix` is encoded: the
  -- knot is tied by the declaration, so there are no mutable cells and no
  -- initialization order. Methods are (name, parameter, body), see above.
  | jLocalClass JIdent (Array (\Sigma JIdent JIdent (Array JStmt)))
  | jThrow JExpr
  | jReturn JExpr
  | jComment String

-- A λ□ constant (`c_foo`) or the program's value (`body`). Only a name and a
-- body: every one is `public static Object <name>()` -- static since the class is
-- a namespace, `Object` since that is the only type a λ□ value has, zero-argument
-- since a λ□ function is a closure VALUE. A closure's `apply` and a `fix` sibling
-- belong to `jClosure`/`jLocalClass` instead.
\record JMethod
  (mName : JIdent)
  (mBody : Array JStmt)

\data JMember
  | jMethod JMethod
  | jMemberComment String  -- what an erased inductive produces

-- `public final class <className> { .. }`, a LIBRARY: `body()` returns the
-- program's value and there is no entry point, so a caller writes the `main`
-- (`runtime/Main.java.in`). The name is DATA so two separately extracted programs
-- can coexist; it says what to call the output, not how to compile it.
\record JClass (className : JIdent) (classMembers : Array JMember)
